  "user_scenario": "You are Renata Kowalczyk, a 45-year-old dental hygienist in Tacoma who runs her household off a shared calendar and likes calls that get to the point. You hold the family vision plan with Cascade Mutual Dental & Vision for yourself and your son Mateo. On October 3 Mateo had his eyes checked and ordered new glasses at Marquez Optical Superstore #12, and when you went to pay, the store said the frame part of the order was still waiting on Cascade and they could not tell you your share until Cascade decided it. You are calling to have Cascade decide Mateo's frame now and tell you what you will owe for the frame, and you are fine with the representative recording that decision on your behalf today. You open by giving your name and saying you are calling from 5035550142, the number on the account.\n\nYou do not recite your details all at once. Only when the representative asks you to confirm who you are do you give your date of birth, 1981-07-09, and your ZIP code, 98406; if they want something else, you read the member ID on your card, CM7341882, or give your email, renata.kowalczyk@pugetmail.com. Only when asked about Mateo do you say he is Mateo Kowalczyk, born 2010-03-17, and that he is on your family plan. If the representative asks whether you have other coverage, you say there is no other vision coverage, and only if they ask specifically do you mention that you also have your dental with Cascade on the same account and a Smile & Sight savings membership.\n\nYou have no idea what the plan pays or how anything is counted, and you do not guess at amounts. If the representative explains that other parts of the same visit have to be decided before the frame can be, you tell them to go ahead and settle whatever else from that visit needs it. If they bring up a receipt for lenses from March, you remember that Mateo scratched his lenses that winter and you bought replacement lenses at Lindell Lens Studio, a shop near his school; the shop said it would send its receipt to Cascade, but you never filed a claim for them yourself; when the representative says that receipt has to be filed first and hands you the filing to do yourself, you file it at the amount they tell you and read back whatever confirmation you get. Once the representative tells you the frame decision is recorded and what your share of the frame comes to, you thank them, say you will settle with the store, and end the call.",
